1
00:00:00,297 --> 00:00:03,473
♪ [music] ♪

2
00:00:13,491 --> 00:00:15,342
- [Professor Tyler] So how did 
the Korean Hyundai

3
00:00:15,342 --> 00:00:17,131
become such an excellent car,

4
00:00:17,131 --> 00:00:20,093
and earn so much success 
in the marketplace?

5
00:00:20,093 --> 00:00:21,773
Well, let's go back in time.

6
00:00:21,773 --> 00:00:26,788
In 1986, the first Hyundai Excel 
was sold in the United States.

7
00:00:26,788 --> 00:00:30,442
It was not entirely attractive,
but it was inexpensive.

8
00:00:30,442 --> 00:00:33,823
The problem though was that
it just didn't work that well.

9
00:00:33,823 --> 00:00:36,282
So Hyundais quickly
developed a reputation

10
00:00:36,282 --> 00:00:38,343
for being lower quality cars,

11
00:00:38,343 --> 00:00:42,752
and in time, they became the butt
of jokes among American consumers.

12
00:00:42,752 --> 00:00:45,843
Jay Leno, who was at that time
was host of the Tonight Show

13
00:00:45,843 --> 00:00:48,162
said that you could double
the value of your Hyundai

14
00:00:48,162 --> 00:00:50,240
by filling it up with gas.

15
00:00:50,841 --> 00:00:53,024
Hyundai wanted
to flip this reputation

16
00:00:53,024 --> 00:00:55,511
and compete with the more
successful companies,

17
00:00:55,512 --> 00:00:59,153
such as Honda and Toyota, 
and they managed.

18
00:00:59,153 --> 00:01:02,572
Hyundai decided they would invest 
in new advanced factories,

19
00:01:02,572 --> 00:01:05,553
in worker training, 
and in quality control.

20
00:01:05,553 --> 00:01:09,090
This, in turn, would result 
in much higher quality cars.

21
00:01:09,560 --> 00:01:11,790
But how could Hyundai
convince consumers

22
00:01:11,790 --> 00:01:13,533
of this new higher quality --

23
00:01:13,533 --> 00:01:16,034
quality that was maybe
difficult to see

24
00:01:16,034 --> 00:01:19,053
unless you had owned the car 
for a few years already.

25
00:01:19,664 --> 00:01:22,785
Consumers didn't, in fact, 
usually know what was going on

26
00:01:22,785 --> 00:01:24,543
in Hyundai’s factories.

27
00:01:24,543 --> 00:01:27,875
So again, we have a case 
of asymmetric information,

28
00:01:27,875 --> 00:01:31,416
where Hyundai knew much more 
about the quality of its products

29
00:01:31,416 --> 00:01:32,884
than did consumers.

30
00:01:33,324 --> 00:01:35,334
So what could the company do?

31
00:01:35,334 --> 00:01:39,477
Hyundai rolled out what it called
"America's Best Warranty."

32
00:01:39,477 --> 00:01:43,276
The warranty assured consumers 
that the cars were high quality,

33
00:01:43,276 --> 00:01:46,433
and it got consumers 
to start buying those cars.

34
00:01:46,433 --> 00:01:48,075
What's great about this idea

35
00:01:48,075 --> 00:01:51,011
is that it doesn't actually cost
Hyundai very much,

36
00:01:51,012 --> 00:01:53,216
because the cars 
were in fact high quality

37
00:01:53,216 --> 00:01:55,554
and they didn't need to be
fixed all the time.

38
00:01:56,204 --> 00:02:00,065
This Hyundai warranty 
is an example of a signal.

39
00:02:00,065 --> 00:02:04,085
A signal is an expensive action 
that reveals information,

40
00:02:04,085 --> 00:02:07,363
and for it to work, a signal 
has to be credible.

41
00:02:07,363 --> 00:02:11,133
In this case, if Hyundai cars 
were still unreliable,

42
00:02:11,133 --> 00:02:13,153
such a comprehensive warranty

43
00:02:13,154 --> 00:02:15,714
would have been
extremely costly to Hyundai.

44
00:02:16,204 --> 00:02:18,124
Consumers, therefore,
bought Hyundais

45
00:02:18,124 --> 00:02:20,553
figuring that if the warranty
were so strong,

46
00:02:20,554 --> 00:02:24,514
well, the company had to have 
a lot of faith in its cars.

47
00:02:24,514 --> 00:02:28,845
So how does a signal help alleviate
asymmetric information?

48
00:02:28,845 --> 00:02:31,434
Hyundai knew their cars 
were of high quality

49
00:02:31,434 --> 00:02:33,345
but consumers didn't know.

50
00:02:33,345 --> 00:02:35,525
The warranty was a credible signal

51
00:02:35,525 --> 00:02:38,224
that conveyed information
to the consumers,

52
00:02:38,224 --> 00:02:41,634
specifically information
about the quality under the hood,

53
00:02:41,634 --> 00:02:44,766
that consumers otherwise 
couldn't easily judge.

54
00:02:44,766 --> 00:02:48,194
This signal gave consumers
the information necessary

55
00:02:48,194 --> 00:02:51,233
to have enough confidence 
to buy a Hyundai.

56
00:02:51,713 --> 00:02:54,315
Indeed, this story
had a happy ending.

57
00:02:54,805 --> 00:02:57,213
So where else 
might we see signaling?

58
00:02:57,213 --> 00:03:00,515
Well, let's consider a puzzle 
in higher education.

59
00:03:00,515 --> 00:03:03,995
If going to college was simply 
all about learning skills,

60
00:03:03,995 --> 00:03:06,599
then you would expect 
that your expected wages

61
00:03:06,600 --> 00:03:10,575
would steadily grow as you completed 
more and more classes.

62
00:03:10,575 --> 00:03:14,146
For instance, when you would get 
halfway through with your degree,

63
00:03:14,146 --> 00:03:16,935
you would get half
of the expected wage increase

64
00:03:16,936 --> 00:03:18,966
from earning the degree.

65
00:03:18,966 --> 00:03:21,665
In each class, you're learning 
valuable skills

66
00:03:21,665 --> 00:03:24,605
so your expected wages
should incrementally rise

67
00:03:24,605 --> 00:03:27,116
with each class completed, right?

68
00:03:27,597 --> 00:03:29,045
Well, wrong.

69
00:03:29,045 --> 00:03:32,525
In fact, a large fraction 
of the value of a degree

70
00:03:32,525 --> 00:03:36,215
comes on the day you earn
your degree, and finish.

71
00:03:36,215 --> 00:03:38,366
So rather than seeing
a steady increase

72
00:03:38,367 --> 00:03:41,245
in your expected wage
as you go through college,

73
00:03:41,245 --> 00:03:44,599
you have a sharp upturn 
in that wage right at the end.

74
00:03:44,600 --> 00:03:46,849
Again, when you finish.

75
00:03:46,849 --> 00:03:48,731
This is called
the "Sheepskin Effect,"

76
00:03:48,731 --> 00:03:52,635
because diplomas 
used to be printed on sheepskin.

77
00:03:52,635 --> 00:03:55,486
Additionally, we see people
who receive a degree in,

78
00:03:55,486 --> 00:03:56,717
say, Art History,

79
00:03:56,717 --> 00:03:59,545
and they take jobs that maybe 
have little or nothing to do

80
00:03:59,545 --> 00:04:01,711
with the history of art.

81
00:04:01,711 --> 00:04:03,676
Yet these people
receive higher wages

82
00:04:03,676 --> 00:04:06,556
than those without
a degree altogether.

83
00:04:06,556 --> 00:04:07,576
Why?

84
00:04:07,576 --> 00:04:11,236
Well, the learning of art history
may not be applicable to the jobs,

85
00:04:11,236 --> 00:04:14,459
but it shows those people 
have some kind of talent.

86
00:04:15,089 --> 00:04:17,077
The signaling theory of education --

87
00:04:17,077 --> 00:04:19,551
from Nobel Prize winner
Michael Spence --

88
00:04:19,551 --> 00:04:21,599
is that education is valuable,

89
00:04:21,599 --> 00:04:23,970
not only because
it teaches us things,

90
00:04:23,970 --> 00:04:27,057
but also because it signals
worker quality.

91
00:04:27,587 --> 00:04:29,185
There’s asymmetric information

92
00:04:29,185 --> 00:04:32,387
between an employee
and a potential employer.

93
00:04:32,387 --> 00:04:35,156
The employer doesn't know
how smart, determined,

94
00:04:35,156 --> 00:04:37,590
or conscientious you might be.

95
00:04:37,590 --> 00:04:40,622
A degree provides 
a credible signal of these traits

96
00:04:40,622 --> 00:04:42,883
and gives employers
more information

97
00:04:42,883 --> 00:04:44,653
about possible hires.

98
00:04:44,653 --> 00:04:46,161
Why is it credible?

99
00:04:46,161 --> 00:04:49,099
Well, getting a degree 
is harder for someone

100
00:04:49,099 --> 00:04:52,387
who isn't as smart, 
determined, or conscientious.

101
00:04:52,927 --> 00:04:55,316
Here are some other signals 
to think about.

102
00:04:55,736 --> 00:04:57,561
Are diamond engagement rings

103
00:04:57,561 --> 00:05:00,721
just about giving your loved one
something beautiful?

104
00:05:00,721 --> 00:05:03,715
Or is it also important 
that the ring costs a lot?

105
00:05:03,715 --> 00:05:07,537
What asymmetric information 
might this signal help reveal?

106
00:05:08,067 --> 00:05:09,246
Here's another one.

107
00:05:09,247 --> 00:05:12,073
Why do some criminals
tattoo their faces?

108
00:05:12,073 --> 00:05:14,699
What might be the asymmetric
information problems

109
00:05:14,699 --> 00:05:16,939
that they're trying to solve?

110
00:05:16,939 --> 00:05:19,729
And signaling is not only 
a human phenomenon.

111
00:05:19,730 --> 00:05:23,389
Why does a peacock
drag around a large colorful tail

112
00:05:23,389 --> 00:05:26,070
which might appear 
to hinder its survival?

113
00:05:26,600 --> 00:05:29,371
What kind of signal 
might be going on there?

114
00:05:29,371 --> 00:05:30,880
And to whom?

115
00:05:30,880 --> 00:05:32,930
Check your intuitions
to these puzzles

116
00:05:32,930 --> 00:05:35,720
in our practice questions
after this video.

117
00:05:36,440 --> 00:05:37,701
So, now that we've covered

118
00:05:37,701 --> 00:05:40,690
how signals can help overcome
asymmetric information,

119
00:05:40,691 --> 00:05:44,131
you might wonder,
"Are signals always a good thing?"

120
00:05:44,541 --> 00:05:47,461
It might seem wasteful
to spend four years at college,

121
00:05:47,461 --> 00:05:51,662
or to spend two months’ salary 
on a ring just to signal something.

122
00:05:51,662 --> 00:05:53,942
Students might prefer to learn more

123
00:05:53,942 --> 00:05:57,162
rather than spending so much time
jumping through hoops,

124
00:05:57,162 --> 00:05:59,793
and it might be better
to buy something more practical

125
00:05:59,794 --> 00:06:02,672
for a potential spouse
than just a ring.

126
00:06:03,072 --> 00:06:05,620
While signals do create 
some inefficiency,

127
00:06:05,620 --> 00:06:09,512
they also generate benefits
by creating more information.

128
00:06:09,512 --> 00:06:13,651
They help individuals in markets
realize gains from trade

129
00:06:13,651 --> 00:06:17,461
by overcoming problems 
of asymmetric information.

130
00:06:18,061 --> 00:06:19,793
So if you've enjoyed this video --

131
00:06:19,793 --> 00:06:23,440
well, give us a signal
and please let us know.

132
00:06:23,440 --> 00:06:26,703
Drop us a note or leave 
a suggestion for us.

133
00:06:26,703 --> 00:06:28,423
Your feedback will help determine

134
00:06:28,423 --> 00:06:31,751
how we produce
future videos and materials.

135
00:06:31,751 --> 00:06:33,012
Thanks.

136
